---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { base } from "../lib/base";
---

<Layout title="For Developers" description="Why Tor Browser Installer matters for the Tor Project and the broader ecosystem.">
  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow"><Icon name="terminal" size={16} /> For Developers</span>
      <h1>Why this project matters for Tor.</h1>
      
      <p class="lede" style="max-width: 70ch;">
        On Linux, <a href="https://flathub.org/apps/org.torproject.torbrowser-launcher">Tor Browser Launcher</a>
        handles this job as a Flatpak: fetch, verify, install, launch. It works, but it's Linux-only 
        and runs Python + GTK in a sandbox. This project does the same thing as a single 
        native Rust binary that compiles for macOS, Windows, and Linux. 
        <strong>This is what a proper cross-platform Tor Browser installer should look like.</strong>
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 48px;">The problem with the current approach</h2>
      <p>
        Right now, Tor Browser installation is inconsistent across platforms:
      </p>
      <ul class="problem-list">
        <li>
          <strong>macOS:</strong> Download a DMG, drag to Applications. No verification, no proper install.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Windows:</strong> Download an EXE installer. It works, but it's a separate codebase from Linux.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linux:</strong> Download a tarball, extract it. Or use Tor Browser Launcher (Flatpak only).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each platform has a different experience. None of them verify by default. 
        This creates a fragmented user experience and makes it harder for users to 
        trust what they're installing.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 48px;">Why this approach is better</h2>
      
      <div class="grid grid--3 benefits-grid" style="margin-top: 32px;">
        <div class="card benefit-card">
          <div class="benefit-icon"><Icon name="shield" size={28} /></div>
          <h3>Verification is not optional</h3>
          <p>
            Every download is verified with SHA-256 <em>and</em> PGP signature against the 
            Tor Browser Developers key before a single file is written to disk. This isn't 
            an afterthought or a toggle &mdash; it's the core of how the tool works.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card benefit-card">
          <div class="benefit-icon"><Icon name="terminal" size={28} /></div>
          <h3>One codebase, all platforms</h3>
          <p>
            The entire pipeline lives in <code>install.rs</code>. Platform differences are 
            handled with <code>cfg(target_os = ...)</code>. Same source, same verification logic, 
            same security guarantees on every platform.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card benefit-card">
          <div class="benefit-icon"><Icon name="lock" size={28} /></div>
          <h3>No sandbox, no runtime</h3>
          <p>
            No Flatpak runtime or permission model to work around. On macOS: mount DMG, 
            copy app. On Linux: extract tar.xz. On Windows: run NSIS installer. Simple, 
            direct, predictable, and auditable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 48px;">For the Tor Project</h2>
      <p>
        If the Tor Project wanted to adopt a proper cross-platform installer, this is 
        the architecture they should use. Here's why:
      </p>
      
      <div class="why-matters">
        <div class="why-item">
          <h3><Icon name="check" size={20} /> It's already written</h3>
          <p>
            The hard work is done. Release JSON fetching, download with progress, SHA-256 
            verification, PGP signature verification, platform-native installation. All of it 
            exists and works today.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-item">
          <h3><Icon name="check" size={20} /> It's Rust, not Python</h3>
          <p>
            Memory-safe, compiled to native code, no runtime dependencies. This means 
            faster execution, smaller attack surface, and easier distribution. The same 
            binary that runs on Linux can be audited and trusted.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-item">
          <h3><Icon name="check" size={20} /> It's cross-platform by design</h3>
          <p>
            Not Linux-only like Tor Browser Launcher. Not macOS-only. Not Windows-only. 
            One codebase that compiles for all three major desktop platforms, with the 
            same verification pipeline on each.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-item">
          <h3><Icon name="check" size={20} /> It can be ported anywhere Rust runs</h3>
          <p>
            Rust compiles to native code on virtually any platform. BSD? Sure, if Tor 
            Browser has BSD ports. ARM? Yes. The code is already structured to handle 
            platform differences cleanly.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-item">
          <h3><Icon name="check" size={20} /> Users already expect this</h3>
          <p>
            Package managers verify downloads. App stores verify downloads. Users expect 
            installers to verify downloads. This tool does what users already expect from 
            any serious software installation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 48px;">For other developers</h2>
      <p>
        This isn't just useful for Tor. The same pattern &mdash; fetch, verify, install &mdash; 
        applies to any software distribution. If you're building a tool that needs to 
        download and verify binaries, this is a reference implementation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The code is clean, well-structured, and intentionally simple. No magic, no 
        hidden behavior, no shortcuts. It does one thing, does it properly, and logs 
        everything so you can see exactly what happened.
      </p>

      <div class="dev-note">
        <Icon name="info" size={20} />
        <div>
          <p>
            <strong>Note:</strong> This is an independent project, not a Tor Project product. 
            It hasn't been reviewed or endorsed by them. But it's here, it works, and it's 
            ready to be adopted or adapted. See the 
            <a href={`${base}security`}>security page</a> for full verification details.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .problem-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    padding-left: 22px;
    margin: 16px 0 24px;
  }
  .problem-list li {
    color: var(--text);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .problem-list strong {
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
  }

  .benefits-grid {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  .benefit-card {
    padding: 28px;
  }
  .benefit-icon {
    width: 56px;
    height: 56px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-radius: 14px;
    background: var(--app-purple-soft);
    color: var(--app-purple-bright);
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .benefit-card h3 {
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  .benefit-card p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .benefit-card em {
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .why-matters {
    display: grid;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  .why-item {
    padding: 20px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--app-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }
  .why-item h3 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 10px 0;
  }
  .why-item h3 :global(svg) {
    width: 1.2em;
    height: 1.2em;
    color: var(--app-purple-bright);
  }
  .why-item p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .dev-note {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-top: 40px;
    padding: 20px 22px;
    background: var(--app-purple-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--app-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
  }
  .dev-note :global(svg) {
    flex: none;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }
  .dev-note p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .dev-note a {
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .dev-note a:hover {
    color: var(--app-purple-bright);
  }
  
  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .grid--3 {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
